message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "GridRescue AI: open-data planning and hourly outage simulation for relocatable clean backup power at critical facilities"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Manjunath"
    given-names: "Pavan"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2694-8807"
  - family-names: "Prüfer"
    given-names: "Thomas"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-10-31"
repository-code: "https://github.com/pavangemmanjunath-cell/GridRescue_AI"
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  GridRescue AI is a web application and Python engine that plans clean backup power for hospitals and
  other critical facilities on weak grids. From a map pin it reads the site from open data (solar, wind,
  water, the nearby grid and the published tariff), sizes nine candidate architectures (UPS, battery
  storage, solar, wind, flow, hydrogen, pumped hydro and generator hybrids), checks each with an hourly
  dispatch simulation through outage cases and four stress tests, ranks them on nine weighted criteria,
  and reports costs, a relocation plan for a facility that must move, and a Scope 1 and Scope 2 ESG
  estimate. A Random Forest trained on engine-labelled synthetic sites gives a first pick that the
  simulation verifies, and a SQLite full-text index answers questions with cited passages. The worked
  example is the National Orthopaedic Hospital, Enugu (Global Energy Hackathon 2026, Brief 12). Results
  are pre-feasibility estimates (about ±30 %).
keywords:
  - energy resilience
